<!--
  PP24: what Visual Studio opens.

  The tree is CMakeLists.txt plus a vcpkg.json, which Visual Studio can open as a CMake folder -
  and a folder is not a solution: no project references, no NuGet, and no F5 with a managed
  debugger attached to the code being written. This is the solution the managed half of the port is
  written inside rather than beside.

  Why .slnx and not .sln. The XML form is what the .NET 10 SDK's `dotnet new sln` now produces, and
  the classic format's blocks of GUIDs are a merge conflict waiting for the first branch that adds
  a project - which this port will do repeatedly as Block F splits the core into components. The
  cost is tooling older than Visual Studio 2022 17.10, and this project targets net10.0-windows, so
  nothing that can build it is that old.

  The native half is deliberately absent. lib/ and the Qt client are CMake and stay CMake -
  compile.cmd remains the only build for them, and it is what produces the chiaki-shim.dll this
  solution's project loads. Whatever of Block F ends up staying native is consumed as a binary or
  as a vcxproj this file references, which is a thing a solution does well; inventing that entry
  before there is anything behind it would be a reference to nothing.
-->
<Solution>
  <Folder Name="/app/">
    <Project Path="app/ChiakiNg.csproj" />
  </Folder>
</Solution>
